      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/JamesTate121/status/2012582281660018747",
      "text": "Russell Johnson’s wartime service was far more dramatic than most fans of Gilligan’s Island ever realized. Born in Pennsylvania in 1924, he enlisted in the U.S. Army Air Forces during World War II and trained as an aviation cadet before becoming a commissioned officer. Assigned as a bombardier on B‑25 Mitchell bombers, he flew missions across the Pacific at a time when low‑altitude bombing runs were some of the most dangerous assignments in the theater.\nBetween 1942 and 1945, Johnson completed 44 combat missions, a number later confirmed by military records and fact‑checking organizations. His service wasn’t without peril: during a March 1945 mission over the Philippines, his aircraft was hit by Japanese fire and forced to crash‑land near Zamboanga. Johnson survived the crash but sustained injuries that eventually led to his medical discharge. His wartime experience earned him several commendations and shaped much of his quiet resilience later in life.\nAfter the war, Johnson used the GI Bill to study acting, eventually building a steady Hollywood career before landing his most iconic role as the endlessly resourceful Professor on Gilligan’s Island. Fans often joked that the Professor could build anything out of coconuts but the calm, capable demeanor he brought to the role was rooted in real‑world experience. His transition from decorated combat aviator to beloved sitcom star remains one of the most surprising and compelling career arcs in American entertainment history.",
