    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2012585687753937100",
      "url": "https://x.com/genuine_man101/status/2012585687753937100",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/genuine_man101/status/2012585687753937100",
      "text": "Ever Wonder How is Life Onboard Large Vessels\n\n Working on massive container ships, tankers or bulk carriers is a unique world: adventure, routine, isolation & real danger. Few jobs put you thousands of miles from home for months, surrounded by ocean, steel & a tight multinational crew.The rhythm? Watch system — usually 4 hours on / 8 off on the bridge, guiding a 300m+ giant through weather & traffic.\n\n\rBelow decks: hot, noisy engine room with engines over 100,000 \n\n\rOn deck: physical work,  lashing containers, painting, mooring in all weather.\n\nLiving quarters: small but private cabins (newer ships have decent Wi-Fi via Starlink). Crew ~20-30 people = tight community.Meals in the mess hall are morale boosters.\n\n\rFree time? Gym, movies, reading… or just watching endless ocean.Highs: Epic sunsets only a few see, visiting ports worldwide, deep crew bonds (Philippines, India, Ukraine & more), great pay + long leave, pride in moving 90% of world trade.\n\n\rLows: Months away from family, constant motion, spotty internet, heavy paperwork, real risks (weather, machinery, isolation).It’s not glamorous. It’s raw, repetitive, sometimes terrifying — and strangely addictive.For the resilient, it’s one of the last true frontiers: high responsibility, global views, solid money & quiet pride.Many seafarers say it best:\n“It’s not a job. It’s a way of life.”",
